    Let us summarize the known parameters s+1, t+1, α for which a proper pg(s + 1, t + 1, α)
with an abelian Singer groups exists. An infinite family was discovered by Thas [20, 21].
His construction is based on PG-reguli which are defined as follows. Let n ≥ 2 be an integer
and let q be a prime power. By PG(n, q) we denote the desarguesian projective geometry
of dimension n over Fq . Let m < n and let R be a set of pairwise disjoint m-dimensional
subspaces of Π = PG(n, q). Then R is called a PG-regulus if there is a constant α > 0 such
that the following condition is satisfied.
    If U is any m + 1-dimensional subspace of PG(n, q) and an element V of R is a subspace
of U , then U has a point in common with exactly α elements of R \ {V }.
